        "math_academy_id": "q-40827",
        "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}?$",
        "worked_solution": "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the angle it makes with the negative $x$-axis is \n\n$$\n\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{8}=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/da2dcf4835abda052f4e7e865fcb652abf5a2d4a482d64ba062e39030e5596c6.png)",
